<asp:Content ID="Content3" ContentPlaceHolderID="ContentPlaceHolder2" runat="server">

    <div id="MainBox">
        <h2>
            Bulma is one of the most important characters in the "Dragon Ball" series, playing a critical <br>
            role from the very beginning in the original "Dragon Ball" and continuing to be a central figure <br>
            throughout "Dragon Ball Z" and "Dragon Ball Super." Unlike the Saiyan warriors or martial artists <br>
            in the series, Bulma's strength lies in her intelligence, ingenuity, and resourcefulness. She is <br>
            not a combatant but a genius scientist, inventor, and one of Goku's closest friends. 
        </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="textBoxes" id="box1">
        <h2>
            Bulma first appears in the original "Dragon Ball" as a teenage girl on a quest to find the <br>
            Dragon Balls. At this point, she is already a prodigious scientist and inventor, having created <br>
            the Dragon Radar, a device that can locate the Dragon Balls with pinpoint accuracy. This <br>
            invention essentially sets the entire plot of "Dragon Ball" in motion, as it allows her and Goku <br>
            to search for the Dragon Balls and encounter various friends and foes along the way.
        </h2>
        <img id="BulmaTeen1" src="/images/BulmaTeen1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box2">
        <h2>
            Despite being a normal human with no fighting abilities, Bulma is fearless and confident, <br>
            sometimes to the point of arrogance. Her initial meeting with Goku in the wilderness showcases <br>
            her boldness and intelligence. She manipulates the situation, realizing that Goku holds the <br>
            four-star Dragon Ball, which is one of the seven magical orbs she needs to summon Shenron. <br>
            Rather than simply taking it, she convinces Goku to join her on the quest, recognizing that <br>
            his strength would be invaluable in their journey.
        </h2>
        <img id="KidGokuWithDragonBall" src="/images/KidGokuWithDragonBall.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box3">
        <h2>
            Bulma’s early characterization presents her as somewhat materialistic and shallow, with a <br>
            particular interest in boys and luxury, but this aspect of her personality is balanced by her <br>
            remarkable intellect. She builds numerous gadgets that aid Goku and their friends, such as <br>
            vehicles, capsules that can transform into houses or motorcycles, and improvements to the <br>
            Dragon Radar. This technological prowess is what makes Bulma essential to the group, even if <br>
            she doesn’t fight alongside them.
        </h2>
        <img id="DragonBallRadar" src="/images/DragonBallRadar.png">
        <img id="Capsule1" src="/images/Capsule1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box4">
        <h2>
            As the series progresses, Bulma's relationship with Goku and the others deepens. While <br>
            initially annoyed by Goku’s naivety and lack of social skills, Bulma quickly becomes a <br>
            caring and protective friend. Her ability to think on her feet and her quick wit frequently <br>
            help the group out of tight spots, whether it's distracting enemies or coming up with <br>
            ingenious solutions to problems.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box5">
        <h2>
            By the time "Dragon Ball Z" begins, Bulma has matured considerably. She is no longer the <br>
            teenage girl chasing adventure for fun. Instead, she becomes an even more vital figure, <br>
            playing a critical role in the series as a mother, inventor, and strategist.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box6">
        <h2>
            One of Bulma's most significant contributions early in "Dragon Ball Z" is her role in the <br>
            Namek Saga. After the deaths of several Z Fighters at the hands of the Saiyans, Bulma builds a <br>
            spaceship to take Gohan, Krillin, and herself to the planet Namek, where they plan to use the <br>
            Namekian Dragon Balls to revive their friends. Her technological expertise once again proves to <br>
            be indispensable. On Namek, while Goku, Vegeta, and others are engaged in intense battles against <br>
            Frieza and his forces, Bulma is left to handle the technical and strategic side of the mission. <br>
            She may not be on the frontlines, but her support from behind the scenes is crucial to the <br>
            success of their efforts.
        </h2>
        <img id="namekSpaceShip1" src="/images/namekSpaceShip1.png"> 
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box7">
        <h2>
            Bulma’s relationship with Vegeta is also one of the most intriguing character arcs in "Dragon <br>
            Ball Z." Initially, she dislikes the Saiyan prince, who, at the start of the series, is one of <br>
            the most ruthless and cold-hearted characters. However, over time, Bulma begins to see beyond <br>
            Vegeta's harsh exterior, and their relationship slowly develops. By the time the Android Saga <br>
            rolls around, Bulma and Vegeta have a child together: Trunks. Bulma’s influence on Vegeta, <br>
            softening him and providing him with a family and sense of belonging, is one of the reasons <br>
            for Vegeta’s growth as a character. She brings out a more compassionate side in him, though <br>
            Vegeta never loses his pride or fierce Saiyan nature.
        </h2>
        <img id="VegetaAndbulma" src="/images/Vegeta&Bulma2.png">
        <img id="KidTrunks2" src="/images/KidTrunks2.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box8">
        <h2>
            Though much of "Dragon Ball Z" focuses on epic battles and the power scaling of the Saiyan <br>
            warriors, Bulma continues to be a key player through her inventions. She creates tools like the <br>
            Gravity Room, which Vegeta uses to train and reach new levels of power. Bulma's brilliance <br>
            allows the Z Fighters to push beyond their limits, and her quick thinking in dangerous situations <br>
            often saves the day. For instance, during the Android and Cell sagas, Bulma is the one who helps <br>
            modify the Androids and figure out key weaknesses that give the heroes an edge in their fights.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box9">
        <h2>
            As a mother, Bulma shows great love and concern for Trunks, both the future version who warns <br>
            the Z Fighters about the Android threat and her own infant son. Bulma's maternal instincts come <br>
            to the forefront, making her more protective and showing a more vulnerable side. Despite being <br>
            more settled into family life, Bulma's adventurous spirit never diminishes, and she remains a <br>
            crucial part of the group's ongoing struggles against powerful foes.
        </h2>
        <img id="teenTrunks2" src="/images/teenTrunks2.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box10">
        <h2>
            In "Dragon Ball Super," Bulma continues to be a key figure, and her character becomes even <br>
            more central to the plot in certain story arcs. With her technological abilities still unmatched, <br>
            she plays a major role in supporting the Z Fighters in their battles against gods and interdimensional <br>
            threats. One of her standout moments comes during the Battle of Gods arc, where she hosts her birthday <br>
            party, which inadvertently sets off the chain of events leading to Goku's battle with Beerus, <br>
            the God of Destruction.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box11">
        <h2>
            What is remarkable about Bulma in "Dragon Ball Super" is how her confidence and determination <br>
            shine through even when faced with beings far beyond human comprehension, such as Beerus and <br>
            Whis. She is not intimidated by their godly status and even shows remarkable courage by <br>
            slapping Beerus after he destroys her party. This act of defiance impresses Beerus, showcasing <br>
            once again that while Bulma may lack physical strength, her bravery is second to none.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box12">
        <h2>
            Another significant development in "Dragon Ball Super" is Bulma’s role as a scientist and inventor. <br>
            Her research into time travel becomes essential during the Future Trunks arc when an evil version <br>
            of Goku, Goku Black, terrorizes Trunks’ future timeline. Bulma not only helps develop the time machine <br>
            that Trunks uses to travel back and forth, but she is also instrumental in strategizing and providing <br>
            critical technical assistance to ensure that the Z Fighters can defeat the incredibly powerful <br>
            Goku Black and Zamasu.
        </h2>
        <img id="bulmaScientist1" src="/images/bulmaScientist1.png">
        <img id="TimeMachine1" src="/images/TimeMachine1.png"> 
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box13">
        <h2>
            Furthermore, Bulma plays a pivotal role in the Universe Survival Saga, where she helps rally <br>
            and support the team that will represent Universe 7 in the Tournament of Power. Her deep friendship <br>
            with Goku is also on full display during this time, as she continues to support him, even when his <br>
            desire to fight strong opponents leads to dangerous situations for the universe. Though not a warrior, <br>
            Bulma’s emotional strength and ability to maintain her composure in the face of inter-universal stakes <br>
            make her a valuable member of the team.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box14">
        <h2>
            One of Bulma’s most notable contributions in "Dragon Ball Super" is her development of advanced <br>
            technology to communicate with gods and beings from other universes. Her ability to bridge the <br>
            gap between human ingenuity and divine power is a testament to her brilliance. She may not be a <br>
            Saiyan capable of godlike transformations, but she is the one who can make those transformations <br>
            possible through her genius.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box15">
        <h2>
            Throughout the "Dragon Ball" series, Bulma has evolved from a curious and adventurous teenager <br>
            into a mother, a scientist, and a key figure in the Z Fighters’ fight to protect the Earth and <br>
            the universe. She is not defined by physical strength but by her intellect, resourcefulness, <br>
            and unwavering courage. Bulma represents the power of human ingenuity in a world dominated by <br>
            Saiyans, gods, and aliens, and she continues to play a vital role, even when facing threats <br>
            far beyond human comprehension.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box16">
        <h2>
            In many ways, Bulma is the heart of the "Dragon Ball" series. Her friendship with Goku is one <br>
            of the longest-lasting and most important relationships in the series, and her influence on <br>
            Vegeta and their family is a key element of the character's redemption arc. Without Bulma, the <br>
            Z Fighters would lack the technological edge and strategic support that often makes the <br>
            difference in their battles.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box17">
        <h2>
            Bulma’s legacy in "Dragon Ball" is that of a brilliant mind, a fearless adventurer, and a devoted 
            mother. While others fight on the frontlines, Bulma ensures that they have the tools, strategies, 
            and knowledge needed to succeed.
        </h2>
    </div>

